From: SF2000 boot project <sf2000@localhost>
Subject: sf2000: pin L1 cache line size and board cache geometry in Kconfig

The HC15xx core uses 128-byte L1 cache lines and a board-provided (no-op)
secondary cache ops table.  The MIPS_SF2000 target previously relied on
MIPS_GENERIC_KERNEL being enabled for the defconfig to select
MIPS_L1_CACHE_SHIFT_7 and BOARD_SCACHE; the config stamp recipe disables
MIPS_GENERIC_KERNEL and enables MIPS_SF2000, so a config regeneration
silently fell back to the 32-byte default (MIPS_L1_CACHE_SHIFT=5) and
dropped BOARD_SCACHE.  On the physical device a kernel built with the
wrong line-size assumption leaves stale cache lines over kernel text,
which surfaces as Reserved Instruction faults (ri-insn-data 0x00000001)
at random text addresses during early boot.

Select the correct geometry from the MIPS_SF2000 target itself so any
future regeneration from any defconfig base produces a working kernel.

--- a/arch/mips/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/mips/Kconfig
@@ -260,6 +260,8 @@
 	select IRQ_MIPS_CPU
 	select MIPS_GENERIC
 	select MACH_GENERIC_CORE
+	select BOARD_SCACHE
+	select MIPS_L1_CACHE_SHIFT_7
 	select NO_EXCEPT_FILL
 	select SYS_HAS_CPU_MIPS32_R1
 	select SYS_SUPPORTS_32BIT_KERNEL
